@ifnottex
@node Top
@top Kubect
@end ifnottex

Kubect is a text focused Emacs interface for Kubernetes.  It delegates cluster
interaction to @code{kubectl}, keeps each browser session scoped to its context
and namespace, and confirms actions that change state or open connections.

@menu
* Installation::
* Basic Use::
* Safety::
* Configuration::
@end menu

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

Kubect requires Emacs 27.1 or later and @code{kubectl}.  Add the package
directory to @code{load-path}, then load it with @code{(require 'kubect)}.
Bind @code{kubect-prefix-map} to a convenient key.

@node Basic Use
@chapter Basic Use

Run @code{kubect} to open a browser.  In a session buffer, @kbd{g} refreshes,
@key{RET} views a resource, @kbd{l} opens logs, @kbd{C-c f} filters a table
column, and @kbd{?} opens a command
menu when Transient is available.  @kbd{C-c C-k} cancels a running read and
@kbd{C-c C-e} shows its complete error.

@node Safety
@chapter Safety

Kubect confirms apply, remote file changes, rollout restart, exec, and
port forward operations.  Contexts matching @code{kubect-prod-context-regexp}
are labelled as production contexts.  Set
@code{kubect-confirm-mutating-commands} to nil only when another trusted policy
layer provides equivalent protection.

@node Configuration
@chapter Configuration

Customize the @code{kubect} group to change defaults.  Namespace completion is
used when RBAC permits namespace listing.  Otherwise Kubect asks for a namespace
name directly.  When working in a Tramp buffer, Kubect runs @code{kubectl} on
the remote host; use a connection local setting for @code{kubect-kubectl-command}
if that host needs a different executable path.

@bye
